# incognito_toggle — end-to-end coverage for Ctrl+Shift+I.
#
# Verifies the action toggles a visible 🔒 segment in the status bar
# (which proves: the kitty kbd CSI-u byte sequence is recognised, the
# binding routes to `incognito_toggle`, the proxy flips `incognito_on`
# and forces a bar repaint, status_text.assemble emits the indicator,
# and statusbar.render lands it in row N). Second press toggles off,
# proving the action is symmetric and the bar reverts.
#
# Why this is worth its own scenario: the visible 🔒 path is the
# user-facing contract — incognito's *recording* effect (history not
# written) is already covered by unit tests. The visual confirmation
# is what tells a user they're in the protected mode at all.

cols 60
rows 8
timeout_ms 5000

spawn $ATTY bash --norc --noprofile -i
wait_for "$"
sleep 400

# Toggle incognito ON via Ctrl+Shift+I — kitty kbd CSI-u for 'i'
# (keycode 105) with Ctrl+Shift modifier (6).
type "\x1b[105;6u"
sleep 400
snapshot bar_incognito_on

# Toggle OFF — same sequence, action is symmetric.
type "\x1b[105;6u"
sleep 400
snapshot bar_incognito_off

# Clean shutdown.
type "exit\r"
exit_code 0
